1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,896
Your alerting is working
perfectly, and that is the problem.

2
00:00:05,254 --> 00:00:09,803
There are probably three to six
actions your AI agent can take

3
00:00:09,812 --> 00:00:12,300
right now that you cannot take back.

4
00:00:12,625 --> 00:00:15,331
Most teams have never
written that list down.

5
00:00:15,763 --> 00:00:20,125
By the end of the next half hour, you
will have written it for your own system.

6
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:25,190
I'm going to tell you which ones I block,
which ones I deliberately let fail and

7
00:00:25,191 --> 00:00:30,564
revert, and what blocking them actually
costs me in latency and shipping speed,

8
00:00:30,905 --> 00:00:32,891
including the one I got wrong, of course.

9
00:00:33,061 --> 00:00:33,811
Hi, everyone.

10
00:00:33,816 --> 00:00:35,362
My name is Artemis Leonarou.

11
00:00:35,576 --> 00:00:40,360
I'm a founding engineer at NoFire AI, the
context and control model for production.

12
00:00:40,637 --> 00:00:44,898
And in this session, we're going to look
at what it actually means to check an

13
00:00:44,898 --> 00:00:49,672
agent's action before it runs instead
of after, where that check goes in a

14
00:00:49,675 --> 00:00:53,830
pipeline you already have, and which
actions are worth checking at all.

15
00:00:54,071 --> 00:00:55,627
Here is the shape of the thing.

16
00:00:55,955 --> 00:00:59,690
Almost all of the safety tooling
we have built around AI agents is

17
00:00:59,699 --> 00:01:01,266
built to tell you what happened.

18
00:01:01,510 --> 00:01:04,607
The alert fires, the log line
lands, somebody reads it,

19
00:01:04,632 --> 00:01:06,038
and somebody investigates.

20
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:09,173
That ordering is not an
accident, and it is not stupid.

21
00:01:09,673 --> 00:01:12,999
It works, and it has worked for
twenty years because for twenty

22
00:01:12,999 --> 00:01:17,829
years, the thing taking the action
was a human, and humans hesitate.

23
00:01:18,095 --> 00:01:22,573
A person about to do something
ir-irreversible pauses half a second, and

24
00:01:22,573 --> 00:01:25,276
in that half second they sometimes stop.

25
00:01:25,758 --> 00:01:29,291
Every incident review you have
ever read has a moment in it where

26
00:01:29,294 --> 00:01:32,433
someone says, "I was about to
run it and something felt wrong."

27
00:01:33,157 --> 00:01:35,085
Agents do not hesitate.

28
00:01:35,205 --> 00:01:36,797
There is no half second.

29
00:01:37,176 --> 00:01:42,048
The action and the decision to take the
action happen in the same instant, and

30
00:01:42,048 --> 00:01:44,051
your alert arrives after both of them.

31
00:01:44,589 --> 00:01:46,083
That is not a monitoring gap.

32
00:01:46,184 --> 00:01:47,890
Your monitoring is fine.

33
00:01:48,298 --> 00:01:51,955
It is an ordering problem, and
you cannot fix an ordering problem

34
00:01:51,994 --> 00:01:53,696
by buying better monitoring.

35
00:01:54,086 --> 00:01:57,274
And, and really quickly, so
you know who is telling you

36
00:01:57,276 --> 00:01:58,883
this, I am twenty years old.

37
00:01:58,892 --> 00:02:03,486
I have been shipping code to production
since I was fourteen, and for about

38
00:02:03,502 --> 00:02:07,208
the last three years, I have not
been the one writing most of it.

39
00:02:07,517 --> 00:02:12,940
So I am not warning you about the future,
I am describing my normal working day.

40
00:02:13,256 --> 00:02:16,941
I learned this e-expensively, and
I will keep it short because the

41
00:02:16,942 --> 00:02:18,839
lesson is worth more than the story.

42
00:02:19,182 --> 00:02:20,711
An agent wrote a change.

43
00:02:20,745 --> 00:02:24,625
That change went through human code
review, and the person approved it.

44
00:02:24,832 --> 00:02:27,233
It went through CI, and CI passed.

45
00:02:27,474 --> 00:02:30,831
Then it ran, and it did something
that could not be taken back.

46
00:02:31,172 --> 00:02:33,660
My alerting worked exactly as designed.

47
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:37,794
It was accurate, fast, and it
was useless because what it told

48
00:02:37,794 --> 00:02:39,683
me about had already happened.

49
00:02:39,954 --> 00:02:43,730
It had already been done, and it had
already been seen by other people.

50
00:02:44,116 --> 00:02:47,433
I spent a while afterwards looking
for the control that failed,

51
00:02:47,838 --> 00:02:49,654
and that was the wrong search.

52
00:02:49,933 --> 00:02:50,788
Nothing failed.

53
00:02:50,819 --> 00:02:54,434
Every control I owned did
precisely what it was built to do.

54
00:02:55,322 --> 00:03:00,238
So I did not have a detection
problem, I had an ordering problem,

55
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:01,971
and that's the whole story.

56
00:03:01,974 --> 00:03:06,423
The rest of the talk is what I did
about it and what you can all do about

57
00:03:06,426 --> 00:03:11,489
it without the incident, hopefully
Before I take anything apart, I want to

58
00:03:11,493 --> 00:03:15,150
be fair to the thing you already have
because I think a lot of talks in this

59
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:17,828
slot are quietly dishonest about this.

60
00:03:18,171 --> 00:03:20,146
Code review is good.

61
00:03:20,527 --> 00:03:25,400
A human reading a diff catches things
that, automated checks might not

62
00:03:25,428 --> 00:03:30,681
catch, especially intent, especially,
"Why is this thing here at all?"

63
00:03:31,151 --> 00:03:32,592
So CI is good.

64
00:03:32,603 --> 00:03:34,038
It catches regressions.

65
00:03:34,046 --> 00:03:35,770
It catches the thing you forgot.

66
00:03:35,793 --> 00:03:38,017
It runs every time, and
it never gets tired.

67
00:03:38,403 --> 00:03:40,836
Monitoring and alerting are good.

68
00:03:41,171 --> 00:03:44,538
They are how you find out about
the entire enormous category of

69
00:03:44,539 --> 00:03:46,256
failures that nobody predicted.

70
00:03:46,636 --> 00:03:50,045
And of course, I'm not here to tell
you that all of these are theater.

71
00:03:50,066 --> 00:03:50,690
They are not.

72
00:03:51,052 --> 00:03:54,250
I still run all, all of them, and
I would not remove any of them.

73
00:03:54,554 --> 00:03:57,486
What I want you to notice
is narrower than that.

74
00:03:57,991 --> 00:04:01,887
Every single one of these
controls produces a report.

75
00:04:02,339 --> 00:04:05,304
Review produces an
opinion before the merge.

76
00:04:05,645 --> 00:04:07,881
CI produces a result.

77
00:04:08,197 --> 00:04:10,289
Alerting produces a notification.

78
00:04:10,602 --> 00:04:14,536
And for a very large fraction of
what your system does, a report

79
00:04:14,568 --> 00:04:18,519
is exactly right because if the
thing turns out to be wrong, you

80
00:04:18,529 --> 00:04:20,262
read the report and you undo it.

81
00:04:20,668 --> 00:04:25,323
The question this talk is about is what
happens you-- when you cannot undo it

82
00:04:25,716 --> 00:04:28,321
because then the report is not a control.

83
00:04:28,598 --> 00:04:29,463
It is a receipt.

84
00:04:30,202 --> 00:04:34,709
So here is a reframe, and it is the
single most useful thing I have got.

85
00:04:35,226 --> 00:04:39,680
When people think about this, they
usually ask, "Is this action risky?"

86
00:04:40,031 --> 00:04:43,506
And risk is a genuinely
hard thing to reason about.

87
00:04:43,866 --> 00:04:45,316
You need a probability.

88
00:04:45,474 --> 00:04:46,544
You need an impact.

89
00:04:46,552 --> 00:04:50,766
You need to argue with people about
both, and you end up with a spreadsheet

90
00:04:50,803 --> 00:04:52,828
that nobody believes in reality.

91
00:04:53,495 --> 00:04:58,530
So you can replace it with a much
easier question: Can I take this back?

92
00:04:58,590 --> 00:05:01,425
And if yes, how fast can I take this back?

93
00:05:02,052 --> 00:05:06,425
That question has the enormous
advantage of having an actual answer.

94
00:05:06,765 --> 00:05:10,784
You do not need the probability, and
you don't need to predict anything.

95
00:05:11,022 --> 00:05:15,278
You look at the action, and you ask what
it would take to reverse the action.

96
00:05:15,702 --> 00:05:17,497
That is a fact about your system.

97
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:21,716
It is not a forecast, and usually,
the person who owns the system can

98
00:05:21,725 --> 00:05:23,564
answer it in about four seconds.

99
00:05:24,078 --> 00:05:27,788
And once you ask it that way,
everything your agent can

100
00:05:27,788 --> 00:05:30,173
do falls into three buckets.

101
00:05:30,486 --> 00:05:33,100
The first bucket is freely reversible.

102
00:05:33,443 --> 00:05:37,932
You can undo it in seconds by
yourself without asking anyone.

103
00:05:38,270 --> 00:05:43,253
Most of what your agent does lives
here, writing to a scratchpad, producing

104
00:05:43,256 --> 00:05:44,998
a draft, reading anything at all.

105
00:05:45,377 --> 00:05:48,434
Second, this bucket should not be gated.

106
00:05:48,473 --> 00:05:51,630
I want to be really clear
because this is where people go

107
00:05:51,630 --> 00:05:53,314
wrong in the other direction.

108
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:59,473
A gate on a freely reversible action buys
you nothing, and it costs you shipping

109
00:05:59,785 --> 00:06:02,295
speed- On every single execution.

110
00:06:02,536 --> 00:06:04,560
It is pure tax on your side.

111
00:06:04,972 --> 00:06:08,006
The second bucket is
reversible with effort.

112
00:06:08,285 --> 00:06:11,574
You can undo it, but it takes
a while, or it needs someone

113
00:06:11,577 --> 00:06:13,517
else, or it needs a deployment.

114
00:06:13,825 --> 00:06:15,689
do not gate this either, I would say.

115
00:06:15,967 --> 00:06:19,017
Instead, just make some
boundaries for them.

116
00:06:19,374 --> 00:06:23,754
Make the change small enough that
undoing it is cheap, and make

117
00:06:23,754 --> 00:06:25,676
sure the undo path is one step.

118
00:06:26,061 --> 00:06:28,343
The work here is not adding a check.

119
00:06:28,397 --> 00:06:34,172
It is reducing the cost of the reversal
until bucket two behaves like bucket one.

120
00:06:34,570 --> 00:06:38,071
And then bucket three is the
changes that are not reversible.

121
00:06:38,267 --> 00:06:41,321
Once it has run, it has
run, and there is no undo.

122
00:06:41,768 --> 00:06:46,253
There may be a follow-up action that
partially compensates, an apology, a

123
00:06:46,253 --> 00:06:50,867
correction, a refund, but the original
thing has happened and it stays happened.

124
00:06:51,221 --> 00:06:54,643
This should be gated before
execution every time.

125
00:06:55,483 --> 00:06:59,223
So let's see what is
actually in bucket three.

126
00:06:59,249 --> 00:07:03,443
This is the part I would like you
to steal directly because I think

127
00:07:03,479 --> 00:07:07,891
most teams have simply never sat
down and enumerated it like that.

128
00:07:08,172 --> 00:07:13,900
Here is my list of things an agent can
do that you cannot take back It sends

129
00:07:13,901 --> 00:07:18,458
a message to a person or to a channel
or to a customer, email, Slack, a

130
00:07:18,458 --> 00:07:20,995
notification, a comment on someone PRs.

131
00:07:21,674 --> 00:07:24,163
Once a human has read
it, you cannot unread it.

132
00:07:24,414 --> 00:07:28,037
Deleting the message doesn't help
because the person already knows.

133
00:07:28,356 --> 00:07:33,402
It publishes something externally
visible, a release, a post, a status page

134
00:07:33,418 --> 00:07:38,721
update, a package to a, to a registry,
a DNS change, and the internet saw it.

135
00:07:38,968 --> 00:07:43,268
It moves money, pays refunds,
charges, and transfers.

136
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:46,366
It deletes or overwrites
something without a snapshot.

137
00:07:46,941 --> 00:07:48,594
And note the second half of that.

138
00:07:48,911 --> 00:07:51,858
Deleting with a snapshot
is bucket two problem.

139
00:07:52,202 --> 00:07:56,629
Deleting without, without a snapshot
is bucket three, and whether

140
00:07:56,631 --> 00:08:00,158
you are in bucket two or three
depends entirely on a detail most

141
00:08:00,158 --> 00:08:01,697
people have not checked recently.

142
00:08:01,976 --> 00:08:07,035
It grants or escalates access because
the moment a credential exists in

143
00:08:07,037 --> 00:08:11,422
someone's hands, revoking it later does
not un-grant the window that they had.

144
00:08:12,095 --> 00:08:14,945
It rotates or revokes a
credential, which is the same

145
00:08:14,945 --> 00:08:16,898
problem pointing the other way.

146
00:08:17,201 --> 00:08:19,271
It merges to a branch that deploys.

147
00:08:19,501 --> 00:08:22,916
If merging to main puts
something in production, then

148
00:08:22,930 --> 00:08:24,585
merging is not a code action.

149
00:08:24,599 --> 00:08:27,191
It is a deployment action,
and it belongs here.

150
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:30,658
It calls a third-party
API with a side effect.

151
00:08:31,086 --> 00:08:35,463
Anything where the state you changed
is not in a database you control.

152
00:08:36,464 --> 00:08:38,085
That is eight categories.

153
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:42,106
When I do this exercise with a real
system, the actual number of distinct

154
00:08:42,122 --> 00:08:46,945
irreversible actions usually comes
out somewhere between three and six,

155
00:08:47,022 --> 00:08:48,934
and not thirty, not three hundred.

156
00:08:49,339 --> 00:08:54,019
And that is genuinely the encouraging part
of this talk, so I want to say it plainly.

157
00:08:54,475 --> 00:08:57,657
The set of things that you
need t-to gate is small.

158
00:08:58,137 --> 00:09:02,084
The reason this feels overwhelming is
not that there are too many of them.

159
00:09:02,426 --> 00:09:06,376
It is that nobody has written
them down, so every action gets

160
00:09:06,378 --> 00:09:08,907
treated as if it might be one.

161
00:09:09,348 --> 00:09:12,333
Now we are going to go
into something concrete.

162
00:09:12,829 --> 00:09:16,723
If you do nothing else from this
talk, if you do this, it takes about

163
00:09:16,723 --> 00:09:22,072
an hour Step one, list every action
your agent is permitted to take.

164
00:09:22,429 --> 00:09:25,518
And I want to be precise
about that word, permitted.

165
00:09:25,775 --> 00:09:29,467
Not what it usually does, not
what you designed it to do,

166
00:09:29,726 --> 00:09:31,810
but what it is allowed to do.

167
00:09:32,050 --> 00:09:33,366
What tools does it have?

168
00:09:33,388 --> 00:09:35,098
What credentials does it hold?

169
00:09:35,377 --> 00:09:38,189
And what does its token
actually authorize?

170
00:09:38,655 --> 00:09:41,480
This step is uncomfortable,
and that is the point.

171
00:09:41,781 --> 00:09:45,545
Most people find at least one thing
on the list they had forgotten about.

172
00:09:45,803 --> 00:09:46,526
I sure did.

173
00:09:46,872 --> 00:09:50,305
Step two, for each one,
answer two questions.

174
00:09:50,649 --> 00:09:52,291
How long would it take to undo?

175
00:09:52,774 --> 00:09:54,397
And who has to be awake?

176
00:09:54,665 --> 00:09:57,646
The second half matters
more than people expect.

177
00:09:58,060 --> 00:10:02,103
An action you can technically reverse
in ten minutes, but only if a specific

178
00:10:02,111 --> 00:10:06,752
person with a specific permission
is conscious and at a laptop, is not

179
00:10:06,766 --> 00:10:08,512
in reality a ten-minute reversal.

180
00:10:08,848 --> 00:10:13,876
At 3:00 in the morning, it is a two-hours
reversal, so write down the real number.

181
00:10:14,205 --> 00:10:17,117
Answers come out as
minutes, hours, or never.

182
00:10:17,449 --> 00:10:21,060
Step three is gate only the never row.

183
00:10:21,606 --> 00:10:22,851
Bound the hours row.

184
00:10:23,111 --> 00:10:25,131
Leave the minutes completely alone.

185
00:10:25,692 --> 00:10:26,532
So that's it.

186
00:10:26,558 --> 00:10:27,653
That's the whole procedure.

187
00:10:27,669 --> 00:10:31,558
Now you have a list of three to
six things and a reason for every

188
00:10:31,559 --> 00:10:35,646
gate you add, which means you can
defend each one to the person who

189
00:10:35,652 --> 00:10:38,122
asks why their deploy got slower.

190
00:10:38,476 --> 00:10:40,779
Now, where does the check live?

191
00:10:40,839 --> 00:10:45,007
And I want to head something off
because whenever anyone talks

192
00:10:45,010 --> 00:10:49,303
about policy and gating, half the
room starts imagining a platform.

193
00:10:49,609 --> 00:10:51,141
You do not need a new service.

194
00:10:51,142 --> 00:10:52,625
You do not need a redesign.

195
00:10:52,853 --> 00:10:54,343
You don't need a control plane.

196
00:10:54,579 --> 00:10:59,240
Every placement I am about to describe
goes in a pipeline that you already have.

197
00:10:59,843 --> 00:11:04,117
There are four places, and I will
go through them in order of how late

198
00:11:04,153 --> 00:11:06,947
they fire, because later is worse.

199
00:11:07,274 --> 00:11:12,550
One is at the call site, a wrapper around
the function that actually performs the

200
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:16,630
irreversible action, the send function,
the delete function, and the payment

201
00:11:16,632 --> 00:11:21,565
call This is the cheapest to build
and the most precise, and it has one

202
00:11:21,566 --> 00:11:23,660
property that none of the others have.

203
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:28,853
It is the only placement that catches
an action taken outside your pipeline.

204
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:32,515
If your agent can be triggered from
somewhere and you did not think

205
00:11:32,525 --> 00:11:37,560
about, a CI check will never see
it, and the call site will see it.

206
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:40,193
So if you only do one, do this one.

207
00:11:40,662 --> 00:11:45,286
Second is pre-merge, a required
check on the branch that deploys.

208
00:11:45,664 --> 00:11:50,289
It is good when the irreversible
thing is the deployment itself.

209
00:11:50,647 --> 00:11:54,905
And the third is the CI, a
job that inspects the output.

210
00:11:55,206 --> 00:11:59,680
And I want to stress the output
and not the prompt or the diff.

211
00:11:59,855 --> 00:12:02,430
Checking the prompt tells
you what you asked for.

212
00:12:02,758 --> 00:12:06,959
Checking the diff tells you what changed,
but neither of them tells you what the

213
00:12:06,959 --> 00:12:09,268
thing actually produced when it ran.

214
00:12:09,569 --> 00:12:12,853
And in my case, the prompt was
fine, and the diff was fine.

215
00:12:13,686 --> 00:12:18,289
And the fourth is at the boundary, a
policy check sitting between the agent

216
00:12:18,302 --> 00:12:20,358
and the external system it is calling.

217
00:12:20,868 --> 00:12:25,185
Most general, latest to fire, and
the one most likely to turn into

218
00:12:25,185 --> 00:12:26,858
a platform project if you let it.

219
00:12:27,069 --> 00:12:29,470
For what it's worth, I use
the first and the third.

220
00:12:29,511 --> 00:12:34,055
I skipped the fourth entirely because in
a system my size, it would have been a lot

221
00:12:34,055 --> 00:12:38,014
of architecture protecting a small number
of calls that I had already wrapped.

222
00:12:38,854 --> 00:12:41,922
And now let's get into what it all costs.

223
00:12:42,353 --> 00:12:46,847
I promised at the start that I would be
honest about what this costs, and I want

224
00:12:46,849 --> 00:12:52,033
to do that properly because I think this
is where talks like this usually go quiet.

225
00:12:52,251 --> 00:12:54,877
So of course, it adds latency.

226
00:12:55,371 --> 00:13:00,114
Every gated action now has a check
in front of it, and that check sits

227
00:13:00,117 --> 00:13:02,209
on a path where somebody is waiting.

228
00:13:02,569 --> 00:13:05,037
It is not free, and you
cannot pretend it is free.

229
00:13:05,363 --> 00:13:07,089
It blocks legitimate work.

230
00:13:07,552 --> 00:13:10,417
This is the real cost, and
it is not a rounding error.

231
00:13:10,738 --> 00:13:14,237
A gate that has never produced
a false positive is a gate

232
00:13:14,237 --> 00:13:15,435
that is not doing anything.

233
00:13:16,077 --> 00:13:20,614
Mine stop things that are-- turn
out to be perfectly fine, and every

234
00:13:20,628 --> 00:13:23,850
time that happens, somebody's work
is interrupted, and they have to

235
00:13:23,853 --> 00:13:25,708
decide whether to override or not.

236
00:13:26,040 --> 00:13:31,122
Also, a badly scoped gate is worse
than no gate at all, and I want

237
00:13:31,127 --> 00:13:33,094
to be empathic about this one.

238
00:13:33,575 --> 00:13:38,035
If your gate fires too often or
fires on things people know are

239
00:13:38,035 --> 00:13:40,447
safe, they will route around it.

240
00:13:40,691 --> 00:13:45,791
They will find a path that doesn't have
the check, and then you have lost twice.

241
00:13:46,122 --> 00:13:50,523
You don't have the gate, and you also
don't have an honest picture of not having

242
00:13:50,523 --> 00:13:53,328
it because on paper, the gate exists.

243
00:13:53,551 --> 00:13:57,959
And Gating everything is its own failure.

244
00:13:58,410 --> 00:14:01,267
It is the most common
way this can go wrong.

245
00:14:01,646 --> 00:14:05,747
Somebody reads a talk like this
one, decides that safety is good

246
00:14:05,747 --> 00:14:10,697
and more safety is better, and they
put a check in front of all actions.

247
00:14:10,997 --> 00:14:13,613
Six weeks later, nobody can ship anything.

248
00:14:13,801 --> 00:14:18,444
The team has built three separate
bypasses, and the checks are now

249
00:14:18,531 --> 00:14:22,638
actively making the system less safe
than it was because all the real work is

250
00:14:22,652 --> 00:14:28,402
happening on the unmonitored path Which
brings me to the one that I got wrong.

251
00:14:28,869 --> 00:14:31,566
When I first did this,
I gated the wrong layer.

252
00:14:31,720 --> 00:14:34,902
I put a check in front of the
agent's ability to write files

253
00:14:35,204 --> 00:14:39,262
because writing files felt dangerous,
and it was easy to intercept.

254
00:14:39,891 --> 00:14:41,901
It was the wrong gate for two reason.

255
00:14:42,270 --> 00:14:44,098
Writing a file was bucket one.

256
00:14:44,393 --> 00:14:49,271
It was freely reversible, I could undo
it in seconds, and I had just made every

257
00:14:49,271 --> 00:14:52,440
single one of those slower for no benefit.

258
00:14:52,694 --> 00:14:57,465
And the thing that I actually cared
about, the message going out to people,

259
00:14:57,809 --> 00:15:03,983
was three steps further down and sailed
straight past it because by the time the

260
00:15:03,992 --> 00:15:08,569
file was written, the decision to send
had already been made somewhere else.

261
00:15:09,277 --> 00:15:13,622
So I had a gate that was
expensive, visible, annoying,

262
00:15:14,007 --> 00:15:15,560
and pointed at the wrong thing.

263
00:15:15,979 --> 00:15:17,079
It caught nothing.

264
00:15:17,172 --> 00:15:21,083
It cost me real time every day,
and because it existed, it made

265
00:15:21,084 --> 00:15:24,006
me feel covered, which is the
worst outcome of the three.

266
00:15:24,323 --> 00:15:29,290
The lesson I took and the reason the
reversibility question is at the center

267
00:15:29,290 --> 00:15:34,213
of this talk is gate the action and not
the thing that is easy to intercept.

268
00:15:34,514 --> 00:15:38,617
These are very rarely the same
layer, and the easy one is a trap

269
00:15:38,636 --> 00:15:40,177
precisely because it is easy.

270
00:15:41,306 --> 00:15:46,475
So specifically, what I gate, anything
that sends something to a human,

271
00:15:46,819 --> 00:15:51,218
anything that deletes without a snapshot,
and anything that grants access.

272
00:15:51,617 --> 00:15:53,946
this is my whole list
for my specific project.

273
00:15:54,170 --> 00:15:59,267
What I deliberately do not gate, and
these are real decisions, is that I do

274
00:15:59,267 --> 00:16:04,041
not gate rights to branches, I do not gate
anything that is read-only, obviously.

275
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:08,469
I do not gate the agent's ability
to open a pull request because a

276
00:16:08,470 --> 00:16:11,853
pull request is a proposal, and a
proposal is reversible by definition.

277
00:16:12,444 --> 00:16:17,009
And I don't gate deploys to anything
that is not production because the

278
00:16:17,009 --> 00:16:20,915
entire point of a non-production
environment is that breaking it is cheap.

279
00:16:21,176 --> 00:16:25,749
Every one of those is a place where
I decided that the check would cost

280
00:16:25,749 --> 00:16:29,857
more than it would save, and I would
rather say that out loud, than pretend

281
00:16:29,858 --> 00:16:31,465
that I have full coverage everywhere.

282
00:16:31,842 --> 00:16:35,838
Now, getting to the end, we are going
to talk about two limits, honestly.

283
00:16:36,394 --> 00:16:38,761
A gate doesn't make the agent correct.

284
00:16:39,218 --> 00:16:42,709
Nothing in this talk improves the
quality of what your agent produces.

285
00:16:43,212 --> 00:16:48,275
It bounds what an incorrect agent can
reach, and those are different problems,

286
00:16:48,305 --> 00:16:50,114
and this is only the second one.

287
00:16:50,459 --> 00:16:52,781
And the system in my story was small.

288
00:16:53,157 --> 00:16:57,427
I'm not going to stand here and imply
I have run this at scale most of you

289
00:16:57,429 --> 00:17:02,874
work at What I would say is that the
thing that transfers is not the scale.

290
00:17:03,351 --> 00:17:04,508
It is the change path.

291
00:17:04,854 --> 00:17:10,156
The sequence, decide, then execute,
then report is the same sequence in

292
00:17:10,156 --> 00:17:14,995
a system with 18 users and a system
with 18 million users, and the fix is

293
00:17:15,010 --> 00:17:17,003
in the ordering and not in the size.

294
00:17:17,348 --> 00:17:23,313
So one thing to take away is go and
find the three actions in your system

295
00:17:23,579 --> 00:17:26,070
that you genuinely cannot take back.

296
00:17:26,462 --> 00:17:29,420
Gate those and leave
everything else alone.

297
00:17:29,802 --> 00:17:31,677
There is no need to do more than that.

298
00:17:32,074 --> 00:17:37,274
The instinct after a talk like this is to
go out and add checks everywhere, and I

299
00:17:37,290 --> 00:17:41,907
have just spent five minutes telling you
that it is the most common way this fails.

300
00:17:42,412 --> 00:17:45,032
Three to six thing, and you're done.

301
00:17:45,595 --> 00:17:46,714
That is everything.

302
00:17:46,748 --> 00:17:50,370
Thank you a lot for watching this
session, and thank you also to

303
00:17:50,373 --> 00:17:52,885
Conforty too for having me here.

304
00:17:53,231 --> 00:17:54,373
I'm Artemis Leonardo.

305
00:17:54,376 --> 00:17:58,889
I'm a founding engineer at NoFire
AI, where we build software for SREs

306
00:17:58,923 --> 00:18:00,647
and people who run infrastructure.

307
00:18:00,928 --> 00:18:04,105
If that is your world or if you
just want to argue with any of

308
00:18:04,105 --> 00:18:05,894
this, you can find me on LinkedIn.

309
00:18:06,104 --> 00:18:10,796
I would genuinely like to hear which
actions you ended up gating and especially

310
00:18:10,810 --> 00:18:12,598
if you find a category I missed.

311
00:18:12,849 --> 00:18:15,105
So thank you and see
you all in the next one.

312
00:18:15,174 --> 00:18:15,623
Bye.

